After watching Randy Pausch’s Last Lecture, I learned a lot about time management and the deeper meaning behind learning experiences. One thing that really stood out to me was when Randy said that time is more valuable than money because once time is gone, you cannot get it back. I also liked his advice about avoiding distractions and focusing on the things that matter most. As a college student, I sometimes procrastinate or get overwhelmed with assignments, so his idea of breaking tasks into smaller goals really resonated with me. It reminded me that good time management can reduce stress and help me stay productive. As a college student and working full-time, this video helps me understand more about time management and how important it is to apply it in our lives. 

Another important idea from the lecture was “head fakes.” Randy explained that sometimes the real lesson is hidden behind the actual task. I can see this in my computer science coursework because assignments are not only teaching coding skills, but also patience, teamwork, communication, and problem-solving. For example, group projects may seem like they are only about completing the project, but they also teach how to collaborate with others and handle challenges professionally. Overall, Randy Pausch’s lecture inspired me to value my time more and to look beyond the surface of what I am learning.
